My FindAdults walker reports 'Found 0 adults' even though I've got two people over 18 in the graph. All three Person nodes hang directly off root.

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

walker FindAdults {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [<--];
    }

    can check with Person entry {
        if here.age >= 18 {
            report here;  # Add to results
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    root ++> Person(name="Bob", age=15);
    root ++> Person(name="Carol", age=25);

    result = root spawn FindAdults();

    print(f"Found {len(result.reports)} adults");
    for person in result.reports {
        print(f"  - {person.name}");
    }
}
```

Zero results, but Alice (30) and Carol (25) should both qualify.

If `check` never runs, the walker isn't reaching the Person nodes. In `start` you have `visit [<--]`, which follows incoming edges of root; nothing points into root, so the walk stops there. Your people are attached with `root ++> Person(...)`, i.e. outgoing edges. Fix the direction:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

walker FindAdults {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can check with Person entry {
        if here.age >= 18 {
            report here;  # Add to results
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    root ++> Person(name="Bob", age=15);
    root ++> Person(name="Carol", age=25);

    result = root spawn FindAdults();

    print(f"Found {len(result.reports)} adults");
    for person in result.reports {
        print(f"  - {person.name}");
    }
}
```

Now root visits its three outgoing Person nodes, `check` filters on age, and you get 'Found 2 adults'.

