```

No output at all. I expected 'Hello, Alice!' then 'Hello, Bob!'.

Good catch, that means the walker never leaves root. Look at the `greet` ability that runs on `Root entry`: it does `visit [<--]`, which follows *incoming* edges. Nothing points *into* root, so the visit queue is empty and the walk ends before touching a single Person. You built the graph with `root ++> alice` (outgoing), so root should follow its outgoing edges:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

edge Knows {
    has since: int;
}

walker Greeter {
    can greet with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can say_hello with Person entry {
        print(f"Hello, {here.name}!");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build graph
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);

    root ++> alice;
    alice +>: Knows(since=2020) :+> bob;

    # Spawn walker
    root spawn Greeter();
}
```

With `visit [-->]` on root, the walker steps onto Alice, prints her greeting, and her own `visit [-->]` carries it to Bob.

