Jac uses the keywords `and`, `or`, and `not`. For the driving check you want both conditions true, so `and`:

```jac
with entry {
    age = 25;
    has_license = True;

    # AND - both must be true
    if age >= 16 and has_license {
        print("You can drive!");
    }
}
```

With age 25 and a license, both parts are true, so it prints `You can drive!`.

`or` is true when at least one side is true, and `not` flips a boolean:

```jac
with entry {
    age = 25;
    has_license = True;

    # AND - both must be true
    if age >= 16 and has_license {
        print("You can drive!");
    }

    # OR - at least one must be true
    if age < 18 or age > 65 {
        print("Discounted ticket price!");
    }

    # NOT - reverse the condition
    if not has_license {
        print("You need a license!");
    }
}
```

With age 25 and a license: the discount line is skipped (25 is neither under 18 nor over 65) and the license warning is skipped (`not True` is false), so only `You can drive!` prints.

Booleans breaking my build:

```jac
with entry {
    is_raining = True
    is_sunny = False;

    print(is_raining);  # Shows: True
}
```

Error is a missing `;` after `is_raining = True`.

Sorry, that was wrong, Jac uses Python-style capitalized `True`/`False`, so your originals were correct and lowercase `true` is an undefined name. The genuine bug is simpler: `is_raining = True` has no semicolon, while `is_sunny = False;` does. Keep the capitalization and just add the missing `;`:

```jac
with entry {
    is_raining = True;
    is_sunny = False;

    print(is_raining);  # Shows: True
}
```

