I have a private walker that creates an Item on root and reports it back. The creation seems to happen, but afterwards the new Item isn't showing up when I query root's children.

```jac
node Item {
    has name: str;
}

walker:priv CreateItem {
    has name: str,
        reports: list[Item] = [];   # the created Item flows back typed

    can create with Root entry {
        new_item = here <++ Item(name=self.name);
        report new_item[0];  # Report the created item
    }
}

with entry {
    # Usage
    result = root spawn CreateItem(name="New Item");
    created: Item = result.reports[0];  # hydrated as Item on the client
}
```

After spawning `CreateItem`, `[root -->]` doesn't include the item it just made.

That's exactly it, the edge direction. You wrote `here <++ Item(name=self.name)`, and `<++` creates an edge pointing *from the new Item into* `here` (root). So the Item is root's *parent*, not its child, and it never appears in root's *outgoing* query `[root -->]`. Use `++>` so root points out to the new Item:

```jac
node Item {
    has name: str;
}

walker:priv CreateItem {
    has name: str,
        reports: list[Item] = [];   # the created Item flows back typed

    can create with Root entry {
        new_item = here ++> Item(name=self.name);
        report new_item[0];  # Report the created item
    }
}

with entry {
    # Usage
    result = root spawn CreateItem(name="New Item");
    created: Item = result.reports[0];  # hydrated as Item on the client
}
```

With `here ++> Item(...)`, the edge goes root -> Item, so the created node is reachable from `[root -->]`.

