This dumps a bunch of typed locals into `with entry`. I'd like it reorganized so those values live as fields on an object with a `show()` method, and entry just constructs it and prints. Code:

```jac
with entry {
    name: str = "Alice";
    age: int = 25;
    height: float = 5.6;
    is_student: bool = True;

    numbers: list = [1, 2, 3];
    coords: tuple = (10, 20);
    person: dict = {"name": "Alice", "age": 25};
    unique: set = {1, 2, 3};

    print(f"Name: {name}, Age: {age}");
    print(f"Numbers: {numbers}");
}
```

Makes sense -- each `name: str = ...` local becomes a `has name: str = ...` field on an `obj`, and the two prints become a `show()` method. One caution: mutable defaults (`list`, `dict`, `set`) as `has` defaults are fine in Jac, so I'll keep them as literal defaults. Here:

```jac
obj Profile {
    has name: str = "Alice";
    has age: int = 25;
    has height: float = 5.6;
    has is_student: bool = True;
    has numbers: list = [1, 2, 3];
    has coords: tuple = (10, 20);
    has person: dict = {"name": "Alice", "age": 25};
    has unique: set = {1, 2, 3};

    def show() -> None {
        print(f"Name: {self.name}, Age: {self.age}");
        print(f"Numbers: {self.numbers}");
    }
}
with entry {
    p = Profile();
    p.show();
}
```

Jac evaluates `has` field defaults per-instance, so each `Profile()` gets its own fresh `list`/`dict`/`set` -- you don't hit Python's shared-mutable-default trap here. The `show()` method prints `Name: Alice, Age: 25` then `Numbers: [1, 2, 3]`, matching the original entry output. Final code is the block above.

