For that, use Jac's `class` (rather than `obj`), which gives you an explicit `init` constructor and positional construction:

```jac
class Animal {

    # __init__ works here too
    def init(self: Animal, name: str, sound: str) {
        self.name = name;
        self.sound = sound;
    }

    def speak(self: Animal) -> str {
        return f"{self.name} says {self.sound}!";
    }
}

with entry {
    dog = Animal("Rex", "Woof");
    print(dog.speak());  # Rex says Woof!
}
```

With `class`, methods take an explicit `self` parameter and `init` is the constructor, so `Animal("Rex", "Woof")` works positionally.

Good question. That's the key difference between the two archetypes: `obj` auto-manages `self` and auto-generates a keyword constructor, while `class` is the lower-level, Python-style form where you declare `self` explicitly and write your own `init`. Since you asked for positional construction and an explicit constructor, `class` is the right pick. Here it is again, unchanged:

```jac
class Animal {

    # __init__ works here too
    def init(self: Animal, name: str, sound: str) {
        self.name = name;
        self.sound = sound;
    }

    def speak(self: Animal) -> str {
        return f"{self.name} says {self.sound}!";
    }
}

with entry {
    dog = Animal("Rex", "Woof");
    print(dog.speak());  # Rex says Woof!
}
```

If you'd rather not write `self`/`init` by hand, switch to `obj` and use `has` fields instead.

