Good example to show `self`/`here`/`visitor`. On the node ability, `self` is the node and `visitor` is the arriving walker; on the walker ability, `self` is the walker and `here` is the current node. Here's the walker side plus matching:

```jac
node Item {
    has name: str;
}

walker Finder {
    has target: str;
    has matches: list = [];

    can on_root with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can on_item with Item entry {
        if here.name == self.target {
            self.matches.append(here);
            report here;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Item(name="foo");
    root ++> Item(name="bar");
    result = root spawn Finder(target="foo");
    print(result.matches);
}
```

Sure -- a `can greet with Finder entry` on the Item node uses `self` (the node) and `visitor.target` (the walker's field):

```jac
node Item {
    has name: str;

    can greet with Finder entry {
        # node side: self = this node, visitor = the arriving walker
        print(f"{self.name} greeted by {visitor.target}");
    }
}

walker Finder {
    has target: str;
    has matches: list = [];

    can on_root with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can on_item with Item entry {
        # walker side: self = walker, here = current node
        if here.name == self.target {
            self.matches.append(here);
            report here;                  # emit to result.reports
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Item(name="foo");
    root ++> Item(name="bar");

    result = root spawn Finder(target="foo");
    print(result.matches);                # [Item(name='foo')]
}
```

Both items greet as the Finder arrives (`foo greeted by foo`, `bar greeted by foo`), the walker collects only the `foo` match, and `result.matches` prints `[Item(name='foo')]`.

