Separate the method bodies on `Animal` from the declaration using impl blocks -- both the `speak` method and the `named` factory. Keep `Dog` inheriting as-is. Code:

```jac
obj Animal {
    has name: str,
        sound: str = "...";

    static def named(name: str) -> Animal {
        return Animal(name=name);
    }

    def speak() -> str {
        return f"{self.name} says {self.sound}";
    }
}

obj Dog(Animal) {
    has sound: str = "woof";
}

with entry {
    a = Animal.named("Rex");
    print(a.speak());
    d = Dog(name="Rex");
    print(d.speak());
}
```

Both move out: `impl Animal.named` for the static factory and `impl Animal.speak` for the instance method. I split the comma-declared `has name, sound` into two `has` lines for clarity while I was at it. Here:

```jac
obj Animal {
    has name: str;
    has sound: str = "...";
    static def named(name: str) -> Animal;
    def speak() -> str;
}
impl Animal.named {
    return Animal(name=name);
}
impl Animal.speak {
    return f"{self.name} says {self.sound}";
}
obj Dog(Animal) {
    has sound: str = "woof";
}
with entry {
    a = Animal.named("Rex");
    print(a.speak());
    d = Dog(name="Rex");
    print(d.speak());
}
```

`impl` doesn't affect inheritance at all -- it's just where the body lives. `Dog` still inherits `named` and `speak` from `Animal`. `Dog.named("x")` would build an `Animal` though (the factory hardcodes `Animal(...)`), which is the same behavior as the original -- moving to `impl` changed nothing there. The entry still prints `Rex says ...` then `Rex says woof`.

For reference, the finished refactor in full:

```jac
obj Animal {
    has name: str;
    has sound: str = "...";
    static def named(name: str) -> Animal;
    def speak() -> str;
}
impl Animal.named {
    return Animal(name=name);
}
impl Animal.speak {
    return f"{self.name} says {self.sound}";
}
obj Dog(Animal) {
    has sound: str = "woof";
}
with entry {
    a = Animal.named("Rex");
    print(a.speak());
    d = Dog(name="Rex");
    print(d.speak());
}
```

