This `create_piece` factory in my `chess.impl.jac` is an if/elif ladder over the six piece kinds. Every arm constructs the matching subclass with the same `color=, kind=, pos=` arguments. It feels repetitive -- can I make it table-driven instead?

```jac
impl create_piece(kind: PieceKind, color: Color, row: int, col: int) -> Piece {
    pos = (row, col);
    if kind == PieceKind.PAWN {
        return Pawn(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    } elif kind == PieceKind.KNIGHT {
        return Knight(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    } elif kind == PieceKind.BISHOP {
        return Bishop(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    } elif kind == PieceKind.ROOK {
        return Rook(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    } elif kind == PieceKind.QUEEN {
        return Queen(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    } else {
        return King(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    }
}
```

Yes -- since every arm calls a constructor with identical keyword arguments, you can map `PieceKind` to the class and index into it:

```jac
impl create_piece(kind: PieceKind, color: Color, row: int, col: int) -> Piece {
    pos = (row, col);
    constructors: dict[PieceKind, type[Piece]] = {
        PieceKind.PAWN: Pawn,
        PieceKind.KNIGHT: Knight,
        PieceKind.BISHOP: Bishop,
        PieceKind.ROOK: Rook,
        PieceKind.QUEEN: Queen,
        PieceKind.KING: King
    };
    return constructors[kind](color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
}
```

That collapses the six arms into one lookup.

Right -- that's expected. Jac's checker treats the result of *calling a value pulled out of a `dict[..., type[Piece]]`* as `Any`; the element type `type[Piece]` doesn't carry through the call, so the return type can't be proven to be `Piece`. You'd have to launder it through a cast, which throws away the safety the checker is giving you.

The clean way to keep it terse *and* type-checked is a `match` on the kind -- each arm has a concrete return type the checker can verify, and it reads just as compactly as the table:

```jac
impl create_piece(kind: PieceKind, color: Color, row: int, col: int) -> Piece {
    pos = (row, col);
    match kind {
        case PieceKind.PAWN: return Pawn(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
        case PieceKind.KNIGHT: return Knight(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
        case PieceKind.BISHOP: return Bishop(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
        case PieceKind.ROOK: return Rook(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
        case PieceKind.QUEEN: return Queen(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
        case _: return King(color=color, kind=kind, pos=pos);
    }
}
```

This compiles with no error. (You'll get a benign lint on the `case _` wildcard; if you'd rather silence it, list `case PieceKind.KING:` explicitly -- but then keep a trailing `return King(...)` so every path still returns.)

