That's what `continue` does — it skips the rest of the current loop pass and jumps to the next iteration. So you detect the even numbers and `continue` past them:

```jac
with entry {
    # Print only odd numbers
    for i = 1 to i <= 10 by i += 1 {
        if i % 2 == 0 {
            continue;  # Skip even numbers
        }
        print(i);
    }
}
```

When `i` is even, `continue` skips the `print`, so only `1 3 5 7 9` get printed.

Functionally they produce the same output here — both print the odds. The difference is style: `continue` is a *guard* that bails out early on the even case, which keeps the "real" work un-indented and reads well when you have several skip-conditions. Wrapping in `if i % 2 != 0` nests the body one level deeper. For this small case either is fine; the `continue` version is:

