Walkers can send values back to the spawning call with `report`. Each reported value lands in the walker's `.reports` list. Here's the collector:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

walker AgeCollector {
    can collect with Person entry {
        report here.age;
        visit [-->];
    }
}
```

The walker begins on `root`, which isn't a `Person`, so `collect` never fires there — you need a `Root` entry to step onto the first person. Once you add `visit [-->]` from root, it flows down the chain and `.reports` fills up:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

walker AgeCollector {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can collect with Person entry {
        report here.age;  # Send age back
        visit [-->];      # Continue walking
    }
}

with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=25);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=30);
    charlie = Person(name="Charlie", age=28);

    root ++> alice ++> bob ++> charlie;

    ages = root spawn AgeCollector();
    print(f"Collected ages: {ages.reports}");  # [25, 30, 28]
}
```

