Can you review this access-control node? I'm demonstrating the different node ability trigger types.

```jac
node SecureRoom {
    has clearance_required: int;

    # Generic entry - triggers for ANY walker (no type filter)
    can on_enter with entry {
        print("Someone entered");
    }

    # Typed entry - triggers only for Inspector walkers
    can check_clearance with Inspector entry {
        if visitor.clearance < self.clearance_required {
            print("Access denied");
            disengage;
        }
    }

    # Walker exiting
    can on_exit with Inspector exit {
        print("Inspector leaving");
    }
}
```

Here's the corrected version. `SecureRoom` keeps its generic `on_enter` and its `on_exit` ability; the clearance gate now lives in the `Inspector` walker where `disengage` is legal:

```jac
node SecureRoom {
    has clearance_required: int;

    # Generic entry - triggers for ANY walker (no type filter)
    can on_enter with entry {
        print("Someone entered");
    }

    # Walker exiting
    can on_exit with Inspector exit {
        print("Inspector leaving");
    }
}

walker Inspector {
    has clearance: int;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    # Access control lives in the WALKER: disengage is walker-only
    can check_clearance with SecureRoom entry {
        if self.clearance < here.clearance_required {
            print("Access denied");
            disengage;
        }
    }
}
```

Now when an `Inspector` enters a `SecureRoom`, the walker checks its own `clearance` against the room's requirement and disengages if it's insufficient — and it all compiles.

